On straffesites.com , every page has exactly one URL, and that URL has no trailing slash: /en/blog/website-without-cms , never /en/blog/website-without-cms/ . The site is a static Astro 7 build (Astro 7.2.8 at the time of writing) deployed on Vercel. Setting trailingSlash: 'never' looks like a one-line decision. In practice it touches five places: the Astro config, the host, the dev server, the canonical tag, and a build check. This article walks through each layer with the code from that project, the output from real requests, and two things I got wrong. One of them is a build check that passed on every route for two months without checking anything. 1. Astro config: what trailingSlash actually controls // astro.config.mjs export default defineConfig ({ site : ' https://straffesites.com ' , output : ' static ' , trailingSlash : ' never ' , }); The important detail is in the Astro configuration reference : trailingSlash sets route matching in the dev server and for on-demand rendered pages. For prerendered pages, the docs say: Trailing slashes on prerendered pages are handled by the hosting platform, and may not respect your chosen configuration. On a fully static site, that means the option does not redirect anything in production. It documents your intent and changes dev-server behavior. The actual redirect has to come from the host. 2. Vercel: one setting, a 308, and the query string survives { "framework" : "astro" , "outputDirectory" : "dist" , "trailingSlash" : false } With "trailingSlash": false in vercel.json , Vercel redirects the slashed form with a permanent 308. Here is what the live site returns: $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{redirect_url}
' https://straffesites.com/en/blog/website-without-cms/ 308 https://straffesites.com/en/blog/website-without-cms $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{redirect_url}
' 'https://straffesites.com/en/blog/website-without-cms/?a=1' 308 https://straffesites.com/en/blog/website-without-cms?a=1 $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' https://straffesites.com/en/blog/website-without-cms 200 The query string is kept, which matters for campaign parameters. Pitfall: the slashed redirect source costs an extra hop anyway Google Search Console reported old 404 URLs in both forms, with and without the slash, as separate rows. So each removed URL got two entries in vercel.json : { "source" : "/seppe-gadeyne" , "destination" : "/team" , "permanent" : true } , { "source" : "/seppe-gadeyne/" , "destination"
